WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.442 --> 00:00:04.778
- If your vision is not
routinely called crazy,

00:00:04.778 --> 00:00:07.980
then perhaps your vision isn't big enough,

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.106
and disruptive enough?

00:00:09.106 --> 00:00:11.689
Are you being busy, being busy?

00:00:12.585 --> 00:00:16.813
Have you lost that connection
to the life you wanted to live

00:00:16.813 --> 00:00:18.086
when you were a little kid?

00:00:18.086 --> 00:00:20.326
You can be a history maker,

00:00:20.326 --> 00:00:23.368
or you can be liked by
everyone around you,

00:00:23.368 --> 00:00:24.840
you can't be both.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.317
- Hello, Believe Nation, it's Evan,

00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:28.008
my one word is believe

00:00:28.008 --> 00:00:29.568
and I believe in people more

00:00:29.568 --> 00:00:31.051
than they believe in themselves.

00:00:31.051 --> 00:00:34.099
And my sincere hope is
that if you see in yourself

00:00:34.099 --> 00:00:37.420
what I see in you, you'll be
able to change the planet.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:38.536
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:38.536 --> 00:00:40.581
today we're going to learn
from best-selling author

00:00:40.581 --> 00:00:42.899
and leadership speaker, Robin Sharma

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:46.291
and my take on his Top 10
Rules of Success, Volume Two.

00:00:46.291 --> 00:00:47.965
Rule number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:47.965 --> 00:00:50.478
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:50.478 --> 00:00:51.406
Also as you're watching,

00:00:51.406 --> 00:00:53.672
if you here something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:53.672 --> 00:00:55.138
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:55.138 --> 00:00:56.400
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.362
can be inspired by it as well.

00:00:58.362 --> 00:01:00.150
And if you leave it within
the first couple hours

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.338
of this video going live,

00:01:01.338 --> 00:01:04.172
you have a chance to win
one of two daily prizes.

00:01:04.172 --> 00:01:05.514
(swooshing)

00:01:05.514 --> 00:01:07.221
(booming)

00:01:07.221 --> 00:01:10.888
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:17.104
(swooshing)

00:01:17.104 --> 00:01:22.016
- Every genius entrepreneur
has an eccentric vision.

00:01:22.016 --> 00:01:24.132
So, they're posibilitarians.

00:01:24.132 --> 00:01:26.454
They are the people who said,
"You know," (breathes deeply)

00:01:26.454 --> 00:01:29.195
"well, here's the way the
industry has always been,

00:01:29.195 --> 00:01:31.840
"and here's the way the status quo is,

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:32.920
"but here's what we're going to do."

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.156
What's coming to the top
of my mind right now,

00:01:35.156 --> 00:01:37.101
as I record this, is Uber.

00:01:37.101 --> 00:01:40.313
So you've got Travis
Kalanick, and literally,

00:01:40.313 --> 00:01:43.806
through that single app, I
mean, they don't own any taxis,

00:01:43.806 --> 00:01:45.647
they don't own any cars, they don't really

00:01:45.647 --> 00:01:47.719
have any inventory.

00:01:47.719 --> 00:01:52.019
But through that single
app, Uber disrupted

00:01:52.019 --> 00:01:56.138
the entire industry of that
level of transportation.

00:01:56.138 --> 00:02:00.304
And so, these people,
these genius entrepreneurs,

00:02:00.304 --> 00:02:03.971
come up with a ridiculously
disruptive idea,

00:02:05.239 --> 00:02:09.612
that is absolutely
eccentric in its vision.

00:02:09.612 --> 00:02:11.368
And that's why people call them crazy.

00:02:11.368 --> 00:02:13.916
And I'll simply say to
you, if you're vision

00:02:13.916 --> 00:02:18.822
is not routinely called crazy,
then perhaps your vision

00:02:18.822 --> 00:02:21.522
isn't big enough, and disruptive enough.

00:02:21.522 --> 00:02:24.859
And we change the world,
when we change the game.

00:02:24.859 --> 00:02:28.146
And Bono is sharing this
story, that I believe he heard,

00:02:28.146 --> 00:02:32.176
and it was the person walks
inside Kanye West's living room,

00:02:32.176 --> 00:02:35.694
surveys the living
room, and cannot believe

00:02:35.694 --> 00:02:39.907
what's on one wall of Kanye
West's (chuckles) living room.

00:02:39.907 --> 00:02:43.324
And it's this giant poster of Kanye West.

00:02:45.048 --> 00:02:46.709
And so the person who is
in the living room goes,

00:02:46.709 --> 00:02:50.107
"Kanye, it's a beautiful living
room, but why do you have

00:02:50.107 --> 00:02:54.274
"this massive poster of yourself
in your own living room?"

00:02:55.598 --> 00:02:59.953
And Kanye replies, "Well, if
I don't believe in myself,

00:02:59.953 --> 00:03:02.335
"no-one's going to believe in me."

00:03:02.335 --> 00:03:05.380
And my take in that
insight is simply this.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:10.025
No-one's going to believe
in your lofty vision,

00:03:10.025 --> 00:03:14.192
no-one is going to embrace in
a role in your mighty mission,

00:03:15.726 --> 00:03:18.265
if you don't viscerally believe in it.

00:03:18.265 --> 00:03:19.571
I mean, no-one's going to believe in you,

00:03:19.571 --> 00:03:20.722
unless you believe in you.

00:03:20.722 --> 00:03:24.969
And Kanye, in many ways, is
a disrupter in his field.

00:03:24.969 --> 00:03:27.606
And Kanye, in many ways,
is playing at mastery,

00:03:27.606 --> 00:03:29.430
in terms of his craft.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:32.360
But that's coming from this
incredible self-belief.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.499
And yes, I understand, some
people will say he's arrogant,

00:03:35.499 --> 00:03:37.724
and I, in no way, am encouraging anyone

00:03:37.724 --> 00:03:39.724
to be arrogant, or vain.

00:03:41.532 --> 00:03:44.003
But the larger point,
for you and I, is people

00:03:44.003 --> 00:03:46.171
on the rise to mastery, and creating lives

00:03:46.171 --> 00:03:49.708
that are legendary, is to
really dial in on that insight.

00:03:49.708 --> 00:03:50.851
Which is, you know what?

00:03:50.851 --> 00:03:52.859
We really do have to have the confidence

00:03:52.859 --> 00:03:56.790
to believe in ourself, because
a lot of times, a leader,

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.427
a lot of times, a business
builder, a lot of times,

00:03:59.427 --> 00:04:02.838
a craftsperson, we will
be in the wilderness,

00:04:02.838 --> 00:04:06.004
we will have an idea for
a business or a product,

00:04:06.004 --> 00:04:09.271
where the rest of the world
says, "That's impossible."

00:04:09.271 --> 00:04:11.951
And we have to be coming
from a place from within,

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:16.109
where our I can is
larger than our I can't.

00:04:16.109 --> 00:04:19.772
You absolutely must do the
interior work required,

00:04:19.772 --> 00:04:22.731
where you have these fiery
levels of confidence,

00:04:22.731 --> 00:04:24.670
where when the world is
saying, "You know what?

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:28.213
"I dismiss your vision,"
or "That is impossible."

00:04:28.213 --> 00:04:32.746
You have to be that lone voice
who goes, "No, you know what?

00:04:32.746 --> 00:04:35.357
"It is possible, and just watch me do it."

00:04:35.357 --> 00:04:38.221
May have read by book, The
Monk Who Sold His Ferrari.

00:04:38.221 --> 00:04:40.889
Sometimes I speak about
Ferraris, because I don't

00:04:40.889 --> 00:04:45.525
have a Ferrari, but I love
the way that Ferrari rolls.

00:04:45.525 --> 00:04:48.052
And here's what I mean by that.

00:04:48.052 --> 00:04:52.219
A Ferrari is, in many ways,
mobile art on four tires.

00:04:54.305 --> 00:04:57.908
A Ferrari, in so many
ways, is a distillation

00:04:57.908 --> 00:05:02.046
of Enzo Ferrari's philosophy on mastery.

00:05:02.046 --> 00:05:06.589
A Ferrari, in so many ways,
is a reminder to you and me

00:05:06.589 --> 00:05:09.829
about what world-class stands for.

00:05:09.829 --> 00:05:12.513
And what I really want to
dial into today is this.

00:05:12.513 --> 00:05:14.648
Did you know that every year,

00:05:14.648 --> 00:05:17.731
Ferrari only produces 7,000 Ferraris?

00:05:19.134 --> 00:05:21.719
And the point of wisdom,
or the powerful idea,

00:05:21.719 --> 00:05:23.896
I want you to really think
about, and deconstruct

00:05:23.896 --> 00:05:26.558
in your journal tonight
after this mastery session,

00:05:26.558 --> 00:05:30.725
is why go really wide, when
you can be singularily deep?

00:05:32.318 --> 00:05:33.667
I'm going to repeat that again.

00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:36.607
But a great company,
and a great performer,

00:05:36.607 --> 00:05:39.335
goes really really deep
on one or two things,

00:05:39.335 --> 00:05:41.945
and that's how you rise to iconic.

00:05:41.945 --> 00:05:45.687
We live in a world, I call it
the cult of superficiality;

00:05:45.687 --> 00:05:49.646
addicted to distraction,
afflicted by interruption.

00:05:49.646 --> 00:05:53.778
So we take the trinity of
our performance assets:

00:05:53.778 --> 00:05:57.710
our mental focus, our physical
energy, and our willpower.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:59.581
Those are the three primary assets

00:05:59.581 --> 00:06:01.389
of a world-class producer.

00:06:01.389 --> 00:06:04.296
And we defuse them, we disperse them,

00:06:04.296 --> 00:06:08.644
around thousands of projects,
lots of little things.

00:06:08.644 --> 00:06:13.121
And yet, I think of the
7,000 Ferrari philosophy.

00:06:13.121 --> 00:06:15.281
They are world-class, and, in terms

00:06:15.281 --> 00:06:19.140
of producing iconic automobiles, vehicles.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:23.370
But they don't want to be--
they don't appeal to everyone.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:27.483
They don't produce hundreds
of thousands of vehicles.

00:06:27.483 --> 00:06:31.325
They want to do a few products,
they want to bring out

00:06:31.325 --> 00:06:35.075
a few things, but do
them really really well.

00:06:36.004 --> 00:06:39.287
And that's really the craftsmen's mindset.

00:06:39.287 --> 00:06:41.862
That's how craftsmen
think, or crafstwomen.

00:06:41.862 --> 00:06:44.868
It's do a few things, but
do them really really well.

00:06:44.868 --> 00:06:46.300
You are an artist.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.883
My invitation to you,
is to produce poetry,

00:06:50.826 --> 00:06:54.993
in terms of your work, that
the world reveals as mastery.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.948
And that is really all about
bringing rigor to your game.

00:07:00.948 --> 00:07:02.613
It's not about doing a lot of things,

00:07:02.613 --> 00:07:04.640
it's about doing one thing well.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:07.603
Because the secret of
genius is not complexity,

00:07:07.603 --> 00:07:09.995
the secret of genius is simplicity.

00:07:09.995 --> 00:07:12.018
You look at any genius
entrepreneur, you look at

00:07:12.018 --> 00:07:15.647
any genius producer, you
look at any genius athlete,

00:07:15.647 --> 00:07:18.058
they're not doing five sports,

00:07:18.058 --> 00:07:20.625
they're doing one sport incredibly well,

00:07:20.625 --> 00:07:22.799
and then putting in the training time

00:07:22.799 --> 00:07:25.199
to be monomaniacally focused, so that they

00:07:25.199 --> 00:07:29.277
can become otherwordly
brilliant at their one thing.

00:07:29.277 --> 00:07:32.715
And I guess what I'm suggesting
to you, ultimately, is this.

00:07:32.715 --> 00:07:36.062
Think about the 7,000 Ferraris philosophy.

00:07:36.062 --> 00:07:38.473
And if you are a business
builder, let's say you're

00:07:38.473 --> 00:07:40.633
a startup entrepreneur, let's say you're

00:07:40.633 --> 00:07:43.144
a Fortune 500 manager, let's say you're

00:07:43.144 --> 00:07:44.583
a professional athlete, because a lot

00:07:44.583 --> 00:07:46.561
of professional athletes are watching

00:07:46.561 --> 00:07:48.190
these mastery sessions.

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:50.855
Let's say you are a mother,
a father, a Yoga teacher,

00:07:50.855 --> 00:07:52.212
or a chef.

00:07:52.212 --> 00:07:54.786
What are the few things
that you could actually do

00:07:54.786 --> 00:07:58.953
in your work, so that you
take all of your mental focus,

00:08:00.455 --> 00:08:02.391
your physical energy, and
the willpower that you

00:08:02.391 --> 00:08:04.784
wake up with every single morning,

00:08:04.784 --> 00:08:07.627
and you really monomaniacally
focus those trinity

00:08:07.627 --> 00:08:10.974
of performance assets on the few things,

00:08:10.974 --> 00:08:15.860
the few projects, that,
when consistently iterated

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:20.551
and improved, every day,
over time, will allow you

00:08:20.551 --> 00:08:24.670
to reach a place where the
world calls you world-class.

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.151
And if you can not only get this idea

00:08:26.151 --> 00:08:30.554
on the 7,000 Ferraris
philosophy, but apply it

00:08:30.554 --> 00:08:34.343
so it becomes a way of being,
you really will own your game.

00:08:34.343 --> 00:08:36.475
When I was growing up,
my dad took out the poem,

00:08:36.475 --> 00:08:39.969
and he translated it from
Sanskrit into English.

00:08:39.969 --> 00:08:44.772
And it was a poem from the
Bengali poet Rabindranath Tagore.

00:08:44.772 --> 00:08:47.103
And allow me to share it with you.

00:08:47.103 --> 00:08:51.270
It simply said, "Spring has
passed, summer has gone,

00:08:52.614 --> 00:08:54.859
"and winter is here."

00:08:54.859 --> 00:08:57.728
"And the song I meant
to sing remains unsung,

00:08:57.728 --> 00:09:00.735
"for I have spent my days stringing,

00:09:00.735 --> 00:09:03.402
"and unstringing my instrument."

00:09:05.802 --> 00:09:07.717
And my brother and I used
to look at it every morning

00:09:07.717 --> 00:09:08.950
before we'd go off to school, but I

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:10.387
never really understood it.

00:09:10.387 --> 00:09:12.082
And so when I got a little
older, I said, "Dad,

00:09:12.082 --> 00:09:15.960
"you know that poem you had
on our refrigerator door?

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:18.461
"What was it all about?"

00:09:18.461 --> 00:09:20.783
And my dad looked at me, he
said, "Robin, that was a poem

00:09:20.783 --> 00:09:23.517
"written by a man who's
heart was filled with regret

00:09:23.517 --> 00:09:25.514
"over a life half lived."

00:09:25.514 --> 00:09:28.390
He said, "Robin, that was
a poem written by a man

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:30.208
"who postponed his life.

00:09:30.208 --> 00:09:32.124
"He always wanted to do something special,

00:09:32.124 --> 00:09:33.939
"he always wanted to do important work,

00:09:33.939 --> 00:09:36.414
"he always wanted to
be loving in the world,

00:09:36.414 --> 00:09:37.924
"he always wanted to be creative,

00:09:37.924 --> 00:09:39.752
"he always wanted to see the world,

00:09:39.752 --> 00:09:42.169
"but he got busy being busy."

00:09:44.125 --> 00:09:46.625
Are you being busy being busy?

00:09:47.606 --> 00:09:51.802
Have you lost that connection
to the life you wanted to live

00:09:51.802 --> 00:09:53.645
when you were a little kid?

00:09:53.645 --> 00:09:56.701
Are you so busy stringing your instrument,

00:09:56.701 --> 00:10:00.275
that the song that your
life was meant to sing

00:10:00.275 --> 00:10:03.192
has died, or is dormant within you?

00:10:04.111 --> 00:10:06.056
And my challenge to you is simply this.

00:10:06.056 --> 00:10:08.815
To do whatever it takes,
before you sleep tonight,

00:10:08.815 --> 00:10:10.724
to reconnect with the best within you,

00:10:10.724 --> 00:10:12.288
that you may have lost.

00:10:12.288 --> 00:10:13.935
Because one of the things
I've noticed is this.

00:10:13.935 --> 00:10:17.366
There are too many amongst
us, walking the planet today,

00:10:17.366 --> 00:10:19.252
who are sort of in a waking sleep.

00:10:19.252 --> 00:10:21.943
They have lost a sense
of who they truly are.

00:10:21.943 --> 00:10:24.365
And your life'll never work
if there's a huge space

00:10:24.365 --> 00:10:26.796
between who you truly are, and the person

00:10:26.796 --> 00:10:28.252
you know you're meant to be.

00:10:28.252 --> 00:10:31.572
Your life'll never be flooded
with energy, creativity,

00:10:31.572 --> 00:10:35.378
productivity, and happiness,
if there is a large space

00:10:35.378 --> 00:10:39.266
between your life right
now, and your unlived life.

00:10:39.266 --> 00:10:42.547
And my request and encouragement,
and challenge to you,

00:10:42.547 --> 00:10:45.698
is truly that you make
things happen, so that

00:10:45.698 --> 00:10:48.716
the person you are, starting
tomorrow, is more of who

00:10:48.716 --> 00:10:50.172
you know you truly are.

00:10:50.172 --> 00:10:52.208
It makes me think of George Bernard Shaw.

00:10:52.208 --> 00:10:54.204
He was asked on his
deathbed, what would you do

00:10:54.204 --> 00:10:56.176
if you could live your life over again?

00:10:56.176 --> 00:10:59.093
And he replied, after a long pause,

00:10:59.962 --> 00:11:04.344
"I wish I could become the
person I know I was meant to be."

00:11:04.344 --> 00:11:07.350
And I suggest to you, it's
never too late for you

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:11.156
to become the person
you've always wanted to be.

00:11:11.156 --> 00:11:13.791
But don't postpone it for next year.

00:11:13.791 --> 00:11:16.248
Don't postpone it for next month.

00:11:16.248 --> 00:11:18.831
Start being that person, that human being,

00:11:18.831 --> 00:11:22.079
that creative force,
that business builder,

00:11:22.079 --> 00:11:26.246
that mother or father, that
athlete, that humanitarian,

00:11:27.225 --> 00:11:31.033
that force of good, that you
have always wanted to be,

00:11:31.033 --> 00:11:32.338
beginning right now.

00:11:32.338 --> 00:11:35.867
You can be a history
maker, or you can be liked

00:11:35.867 --> 00:11:37.535
by everyone around you.

00:11:37.535 --> 00:11:39.065
You can't be both.

00:11:39.065 --> 00:11:43.382
I mean, the very nature of
living your personal greatness,

00:11:43.382 --> 00:11:48.220
and doing something otherworldly
in our world of ours,

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.325
means you're going to
have to think differently

00:11:50.325 --> 00:11:51.818
from the majority.

00:11:51.818 --> 00:11:54.994
You're going to have to
install the habs and routines

00:11:54.994 --> 00:11:57.306
that most people don't do.

00:11:57.306 --> 00:12:01.473
You're going to have to live,
talk, breathe, walk, work,

00:12:02.339 --> 00:12:06.274
produce, create in a way that most people,

00:12:06.274 --> 00:12:09.925
who are card-carrying members
of the cult of mediocrity,

00:12:09.925 --> 00:12:11.746
just don't buy into.

00:12:11.746 --> 00:12:14.590
When you look at most people
out on the world today,

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:17.864
and this is not judging,
this is just reporting,

00:12:17.864 --> 00:12:20.657
but they're addicted to
entertainment, they love gossiping,

00:12:20.657 --> 00:12:23.212
they're negative, they're
toxic, anyone who wants

00:12:23.212 --> 00:12:26.117
to do anything great, they
want to bring them down,

00:12:26.117 --> 00:12:28.636
they dismiss the
game-changers, and they're

00:12:28.636 --> 00:12:30.661
just coasting through life.

00:12:30.661 --> 00:12:34.225
And so the very nature of
you stepping up your game,

00:12:34.225 --> 00:12:37.178
living your greatest potential,

00:12:37.178 --> 00:12:41.134
owning your craft, dominating your field,

00:12:41.134 --> 00:12:43.169
and living a life that's
legendary, means you're going to

00:12:43.169 --> 00:12:47.607
have to leave the 5%, and make a decision,

00:12:47.607 --> 00:12:49.224
to live as very few people do.

00:12:49.224 --> 00:12:50.376
And what does that mean?

00:12:50.376 --> 00:12:52.274
It means you're going to
be laughed at, it means

00:12:52.274 --> 00:12:54.976
you're going to be ridiculed,
it means you're going

00:12:54.976 --> 00:12:59.022
to be misunderstood, because
leadership is a lonely sport,

00:12:59.022 --> 00:13:00.978
and it means you just might be hated.

00:13:00.978 --> 00:13:03.748
And so there is great value
when people start to hate you.

00:13:03.748 --> 00:13:06.554
Because if you look at Jay-Z, I mean I was

00:13:06.554 --> 00:13:09.779
reading his biography, and he
said, "You know, I really knew

00:13:09.779 --> 00:13:13.158
"that I was having influence
in the popular culture,

00:13:13.158 --> 00:13:14.967
"when people started hating me."

00:13:14.967 --> 00:13:16.875
If you look a lot of the great authors,

00:13:16.875 --> 00:13:20.206
when their books go to
number one globally,

00:13:20.206 --> 00:13:22.336
a lot of the amateurs
actually start to say,

00:13:22.336 --> 00:13:24.887
"Oh, that's garbage, I
could put them down."

00:13:24.887 --> 00:13:27.821
I watched a documentary
last night on Steve Aoki,

00:13:27.821 --> 00:13:31.238
the famous EDM, electric dance music, DJ,

00:13:32.352 --> 00:13:35.089
who is giving so much traction
in the world right now,

00:13:35.089 --> 00:13:37.679
and he was talking about
some of the comments

00:13:37.679 --> 00:13:39.688
that he sees in the social media.

00:13:39.688 --> 00:13:42.726
And it was things like,
garbage, and boring,

00:13:42.726 --> 00:13:46.011
and amateurish music, and whatever.

00:13:46.011 --> 00:13:48.413
Even though he's so skilled,
I mean, you just watch

00:13:48.413 --> 00:13:52.413
the documentary called
I'll Sleep When I'm Dead,

00:13:54.848 --> 00:13:56.288
and you just see the skill.

00:13:56.288 --> 00:13:58.347
And you can just look
at any great athlete,

00:13:58.347 --> 00:14:00.537
and you could look at any great scientist,

00:14:00.537 --> 00:14:03.162
you could look at any
great chef, you could look

00:14:03.162 --> 00:14:07.329
at any great artist, you could
look at any great author,

00:14:08.461 --> 00:14:11.199
you could look at any great
statesman, you could look

00:14:11.199 --> 00:14:14.194
at any world-class stateswoman.

00:14:14.194 --> 00:14:18.331
Anyone who has done something
at an exceptional level,

00:14:18.331 --> 00:14:20.748
breeds cynics and dismissers.

00:14:21.863 --> 00:14:24.496
So, the point is simply
this, the very nature

00:14:24.496 --> 00:14:27.888
of doing something that very
few people are willing to do,

00:14:27.888 --> 00:14:31.154
means that the majority will laugh at you.

00:14:31.154 --> 00:14:32.053
Why?

00:14:32.053 --> 00:14:35.636
Because deep inside, they
know they have the opportunity

00:14:35.636 --> 00:14:38.226
to do something great, too.

00:14:38.226 --> 00:14:42.112
And your example makes them feel guilty.

00:14:42.112 --> 00:14:44.389
And all I'm suggesting to you,
with great love and respect,

00:14:44.389 --> 00:14:45.570
is simply this.

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:47.002
You do have a choice.

00:14:47.002 --> 00:14:50.752
You can listen to your haters,
you can be one of the haters,

00:14:50.752 --> 00:14:53.990
you can join the victim
herd, you can dismiss people

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:57.085
who are doing great things,
or you can reach into yourself

00:14:57.085 --> 00:14:59.434
and ask yourself this
fundamental question:

00:14:59.434 --> 00:15:02.402
what do I want to stand for
for the rest of my life?

00:15:02.402 --> 00:15:05.120
Do I really want to rise to mastery?

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:08.186
Do I want to get to the
last hour of my last day,

00:15:08.186 --> 00:15:12.353
and know that I took every
ounce of the human potential

00:15:13.192 --> 00:15:15.927
I was blessed with, and through easy times

00:15:15.927 --> 00:15:20.867
and difficult times, I
brought it on, and as a result

00:15:20.867 --> 00:15:25.034
of my values, my mindsets,
my heartset, my habits,

00:15:26.644 --> 00:15:30.811
my routines, my disciplines,
I was-- I owned my game,

00:15:33.152 --> 00:15:37.319
and I modeled possibility,
and I served lots of people,

00:15:38.502 --> 00:15:41.972
and I built a great lifestyle,
and now it's the last hour

00:15:41.972 --> 00:15:46.896
of my last day, and I'm
surrounded by my loved ones,

00:15:46.896 --> 00:15:48.896
and even though people threw stones at me,

00:15:48.896 --> 00:15:53.063
I took those stones, and
rather than being knocked down,

00:15:53.968 --> 00:15:57.885
I constructed a monument
of mastery that stands

00:15:59.525 --> 00:16:00.608
as my legacy.

00:16:01.967 --> 00:16:05.873
And if you live like that,
you'll be one of the great ones.

00:16:05.873 --> 00:16:07.728
Which is my greatest wish for you.

00:16:07.728 --> 00:16:10.805
You're in a restaurant, the
server generally gives you

00:16:10.805 --> 00:16:13.979
what you expect, or less
than what you expect.

00:16:13.979 --> 00:16:17.607
You go into a clothing
store, it's what you expect,

00:16:17.607 --> 00:16:19.409
or less than what you expect.

00:16:19.409 --> 00:16:23.681
You do business with any
organization, it's generally

00:16:23.681 --> 00:16:27.848
delivering on what you expect,
but more often, even less.

00:16:29.269 --> 00:16:32.462
So, you have a gorgeous
opportunity to lead the field

00:16:32.462 --> 00:16:35.480
in everything that you
do, by doing one thing.

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:39.191
Practicing every single
day, because it's a muscle,

00:16:39.191 --> 00:16:41.323
the ritual of overdelivering.

00:16:41.323 --> 00:16:43.793
Don't just give your
customers what they expect,

00:16:43.793 --> 00:16:47.153
give them 10X more than what they expect.

00:16:47.153 --> 00:16:50.629
Don't just give your
organization what your manager,

00:16:50.629 --> 00:16:54.329
what your boss, what the
organization is expecting from you,

00:16:54.329 --> 00:16:56.929
show something that is so rare

00:16:56.929 --> 00:17:00.226
in today's economy: initiative.

00:17:00.226 --> 00:17:01.608
Initiative.

00:17:01.608 --> 00:17:04.704
So, you're asked to do a project,
give them five more things

00:17:04.704 --> 00:17:05.971
than they expect.

00:17:05.971 --> 00:17:08.499
You're asked to give a
presentation on something,

00:17:08.499 --> 00:17:10.265
go the extra mile.

00:17:10.265 --> 00:17:14.809
Consistently dial in the
daily habit of overdelivering

00:17:14.809 --> 00:17:16.545
on people's expectations.

00:17:16.545 --> 00:17:17.420
And guess what?

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:20.341
You become a merchant of wow.

00:17:20.341 --> 00:17:24.347
You become indispensable
to your organization.

00:17:24.347 --> 00:17:25.769
I wrote a book a little while ago

00:17:25.769 --> 00:17:28.091
called The Greatness Guide,
and one of the chapters

00:17:28.091 --> 00:17:31.860
talked about the importance
of being indispensable.

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:34.596
And I learnt that when
I was a young lawyer.

00:17:34.596 --> 00:17:36.685
And I remember talking to my mentor.

00:17:36.685 --> 00:17:38.539
And I asked my mentor,
you know the top litigator

00:17:38.539 --> 00:17:41.680
at this firm, how come he is the icon

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:44.930
of the legal profession in this region?

00:17:45.767 --> 00:17:48.204
And my mentor looked at me
and said, "Robin, because

00:17:48.204 --> 00:17:51.686
"that person is
indispensable to this firm."

00:17:51.686 --> 00:17:53.268
Well how do you do that?

00:17:53.268 --> 00:17:57.031
By practicing the ritual of
overdelivering every single day

00:17:57.031 --> 00:17:58.733
for the rest of your life.

00:17:58.733 --> 00:18:01.774
Be yourself, everyone else is taken.

00:18:01.774 --> 00:18:04.895
Well that really speaks to the
power of being an original.

00:18:04.895 --> 00:18:08.198
It speaks to this concept of authenticity.

00:18:08.198 --> 00:18:09.856
Being yourself.

00:18:09.856 --> 00:18:12.538
I mean, we live in a world
where there has never been

00:18:12.538 --> 00:18:16.043
so much of a seduction
to be like everyone else.

00:18:16.043 --> 00:18:18.969
There's never been so much
influence, and hypnosis,

00:18:18.969 --> 00:18:22.226
through the social media,
and through society,

00:18:22.226 --> 00:18:25.277
to talk in a certain way,
to walk in a certain way,

00:18:25.277 --> 00:18:28.714
to buy certain things,
to measure your success

00:18:28.714 --> 00:18:30.774
by the same philosophy.

00:18:30.774 --> 00:18:32.441
And what I'm encouraging
you to do, based on

00:18:32.441 --> 00:18:35.330
this Oscar Wilde quote, is to be yourself.

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:40.179
I mean, talk your talk, walk
your walk, take the time

00:18:40.179 --> 00:18:44.258
to reflect on your dream,
be true to your own values.

00:18:44.258 --> 00:18:47.408
Because life's greatest
heartbreak is self-betrayal.

00:18:47.408 --> 00:18:52.293
Make your I can more important
than your even your IQ.

00:18:52.293 --> 00:18:53.707
Now you're saying, "Robin,
what are you talking

00:18:53.707 --> 00:18:54.854
"about right here?"

00:18:54.854 --> 00:18:57.417
Well a lot of people get stuck

00:18:57.417 --> 00:19:01.351
in victimhood versus leadership,
by the seduction of saying,

00:19:01.351 --> 00:19:03.021
"Well, I'm not smart enough.

00:19:03.021 --> 00:19:05.562
"I'm not intelligent enough."

00:19:05.562 --> 00:19:08.487
Well, you are intelligent enough.

00:19:08.487 --> 00:19:13.239
What's even more important is
your mindset of possibility.

00:19:13.239 --> 00:19:16.621
You see, if you look at the
best athletes in the world,

00:19:16.621 --> 00:19:18.592
the best musicians in the
world, the best entrepreneurs

00:19:18.592 --> 00:19:20.770
in the world, the best
managers in the world,

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:23.665
the best CEOs in the world,
the best coders in the world,

00:19:23.665 --> 00:19:26.939
the best pizza makers in the
world, the best mums and pops,

00:19:26.939 --> 00:19:29.171
et cetera, et cetera.

00:19:29.171 --> 00:19:31.880
Those who get a le--
level results, are not

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:35.588
the most intelligent, or
naturally gifted people

00:19:35.588 --> 00:19:37.801
in their field, but these
are the people that have

00:19:37.801 --> 00:19:41.968
that mindset of possibility,
and it's all about I can.

00:19:43.147 --> 00:19:46.188
And that might sound like a
bit of a platitude, you know,

00:19:46.188 --> 00:19:50.864
having an attitude of I can,
a mindset of possibility,

00:19:50.864 --> 00:19:53.573
a mentality of legendary.

00:19:53.573 --> 00:19:56.829
But when you really deconstruct
it, it's really true.

00:19:56.829 --> 00:19:59.244
The way you begin each
day, sets up the way

00:19:59.244 --> 00:20:01.445
you live each day.

00:20:01.445 --> 00:20:04.910
I mean, the Spartan
warriors said it so well.

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:08.151
Sweat more in training, bleed less in war.

00:20:08.151 --> 00:20:12.145
And if you want to be, literally
undefeatable in business,

00:20:12.145 --> 00:20:16.246
literally unstoppable
in life, you really want

00:20:16.246 --> 00:20:19.467
to dial in your morning routine.

00:20:19.467 --> 00:20:23.348
And so you want to do things
like starting the day sweating,

00:20:23.348 --> 00:20:25.066
because that releases BDNF,

00:20:25.066 --> 00:20:27.468
brain-derived neurotrophic factor,

00:20:27.468 --> 00:20:30.098
that repairs brain cells from stress.

00:20:30.098 --> 00:20:31.870
You want to sweat because
that's going to increase

00:20:31.870 --> 00:20:34.532
your metabolic rate, which
is going to give you energy.

00:20:34.532 --> 00:20:36.406
And we live in a world where energy

00:20:36.406 --> 00:20:39.624
is even more valuable than intelligence.

00:20:39.624 --> 00:20:42.479
You want to, in your morning
routine, take the time

00:20:42.479 --> 00:20:45.067
to write in a journal, so
you actually can detect

00:20:45.067 --> 00:20:48.487
your values, where you connect
with your goals each day,

00:20:48.487 --> 00:20:51.914
get to know yourself even
more, because awareness

00:20:51.914 --> 00:20:54.319
is the beginning of transformation.

00:20:54.319 --> 00:20:56.254
And you want to get your
morning routine right,

00:20:56.254 --> 00:21:00.062
so you take some time at the
front-end of your day to learn,

00:21:00.062 --> 00:21:01.978
because as you learn more, you know you're

00:21:01.978 --> 00:21:03.426
going to achieve more.

00:21:03.426 --> 00:21:07.242
And as you understand better,
you can achieve larger.

00:21:07.242 --> 00:21:10.886
For the past number of years,
I've been reading obituaries.

00:21:10.886 --> 00:21:12.649
And you might say, "Robin,
that's negative," and I

00:21:12.649 --> 00:21:15.133
don't think it's negative, I
think it's really positive.

00:21:15.133 --> 00:21:19.002
Because I think few things are
as powerful to wake you up,

00:21:19.002 --> 00:21:21.448
to remind you about what's most important,

00:21:21.448 --> 00:21:25.516
to fuel your energy, to
get you into the game,

00:21:25.516 --> 00:21:29.751
so you let go of your fears,
and you do what you need to do

00:21:29.751 --> 00:21:31.444
to get big things done,

00:21:31.444 --> 00:21:34.214
than connecting to your own mortality.

00:21:34.214 --> 00:21:38.735
When you realize that every
single day could be your last,

00:21:38.735 --> 00:21:41.969
or when you appreciate that,
no matter how long you live,

00:21:41.969 --> 00:21:45.473
life is a short ride, you just
sort of let go of the fears,

00:21:45.473 --> 00:21:48.475
and you let go of the
limits, and you live full-on,

00:21:48.475 --> 00:21:52.380
and you seize opportunities,
and you have real conversations

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:54.784
with real people, and you
sort of wear your heart

00:21:54.784 --> 00:21:57.090
on your sleeve, and you
bring on the fullness

00:21:57.090 --> 00:22:00.671
of your authenticity, and
you do whatever it takes

00:22:00.671 --> 00:22:03.549
to get your loftiest dreams to get done.

00:22:03.549 --> 00:22:05.438
And so I read these obituaries.

00:22:05.438 --> 00:22:09.453
And it reminds me about
what life is all about.

00:22:09.453 --> 00:22:14.068
And relatively recently, I've
started walking in cemeteries.

00:22:14.068 --> 00:22:15.888
I was in Mauritius a little while ago.

00:22:15.888 --> 00:22:17.445
It's a-- I don't know if
you've been to Mauritius,

00:22:17.445 --> 00:22:19.892
but it's this paradise on Earth.

00:22:19.892 --> 00:22:22.875
It's this tiny island, about four hours

00:22:22.875 --> 00:22:24.682
off the coast of Cape Town.

00:22:24.682 --> 00:22:26.196
And I've been to Mauritius
a number of times.

00:22:26.196 --> 00:22:28.286
I was there to give a
big leadership event,

00:22:28.286 --> 00:22:30.734
I talked about leading without
a title, so it was filled

00:22:30.734 --> 00:22:34.998
with iconic CEOs, and people
running big industries,

00:22:34.998 --> 00:22:39.165
and big companies, and
entrepreneurs, and startup people,

00:22:40.490 --> 00:22:42.376
and it was really wonderful.

00:22:42.376 --> 00:22:45.589
But I also took about a week
and a half, just to refuel,

00:22:45.589 --> 00:22:48.043
and to work on my new
book, and to reconnect

00:22:48.043 --> 00:22:52.444
with my values, and to set
myself up for the coming months.

00:22:52.444 --> 00:22:56.487
And one of the things I did,
was I went for a long walk.

00:22:56.487 --> 00:22:58.960
I love walking, I find
interesting-- like I find

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:00.067
a lot of the most creative people,

00:23:00.067 --> 00:23:02.507
the most productive people in the planet,

00:23:02.507 --> 00:23:04.667
they all have one thing in
common, they have the routine

00:23:04.667 --> 00:23:07.067
of walking on a regular basis.

00:23:07.067 --> 00:23:08.623
And so, I love walking.

00:23:08.623 --> 00:23:11.505
I've walked in nature for many many years.

00:23:11.505 --> 00:23:14.647
And so here I am in Mauritius,
and I just started walking.

00:23:14.647 --> 00:23:17.749
And I was actually
listening to an interview

00:23:17.749 --> 00:23:20.499
of Charlie Rose and Steve Martin,

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:23.532
and it was done in New York,
in front of an audience

00:23:23.532 --> 00:23:25.830
of a few thousand people, and it was about

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:27.333
an hour and a half long.

00:23:27.333 --> 00:23:29.518
And Steve Martin was
talking about his career,

00:23:29.518 --> 00:23:31.642
and Charlie Rose is one
of the great interviewers

00:23:31.642 --> 00:23:36.059
of our time, so he was asking
about how he became so good

00:23:36.059 --> 00:23:39.594
at what he did, and his
life, and his love of art,

00:23:39.594 --> 00:23:42.312
and stand-up comedy, and acting.

00:23:42.312 --> 00:23:44.453
I mean, he-- Steve Martin
became an actor after

00:23:44.453 --> 00:23:47.477
a stand-up comedian, et cetera.

00:23:47.477 --> 00:23:50.102
And so I'm walking, and the
Sun is setting, and I'm walking

00:23:50.102 --> 00:23:54.269
along the Indian Ocean, and
I'm looking at the flowers.

00:23:56.041 --> 00:23:58.299
And I get to this little village.

00:23:58.299 --> 00:24:00.216
And it's this really...

00:24:03.256 --> 00:24:06.827
this little village
with a lot of character.

00:24:06.827 --> 00:24:09.329
And as I'm walking along
the edge of the village,

00:24:09.329 --> 00:24:13.162
still close to the
seaside, I spot a cemetery.

00:24:15.484 --> 00:24:17.988
And I've learned to follow my instincts

00:24:17.988 --> 00:24:19.685
more than ever before, because instinct

00:24:19.685 --> 00:24:23.402
is more powerful than reason,
and life will lead you

00:24:23.402 --> 00:24:27.758
to an interesting place,
if you stay with the flow.

00:24:27.758 --> 00:24:31.618
And so, I walked towards
this cemetery, and I walked

00:24:31.618 --> 00:24:33.265
into this cemetery.

00:24:33.265 --> 00:24:35.830
And I started walking
through the cemetery,

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:39.195
very respectfully and carefully.

00:24:39.195 --> 00:24:41.633
But it was interesting
to me, because a lot

00:24:41.633 --> 00:24:45.069
of the tombstones were
just made out of wood.

00:24:45.069 --> 00:24:49.928
And (clears throat)
somewhat sadly, some of

00:24:49.928 --> 00:24:54.104
the wooden crosses, were
actually really really small.

00:24:54.104 --> 00:24:58.271
And you could tell that the
gravesites were of kids.

00:24:59.747 --> 00:25:03.822
And as I walked through the
cemetery, I saw these crosses,

00:25:03.822 --> 00:25:08.458
and I saw some tombstones,
some looked very old,

00:25:08.458 --> 00:25:10.625
a few graves looked fresh.

00:25:11.829 --> 00:25:15.996
And it reminded me, really,
about the shortness of life.

00:25:16.987 --> 00:25:20.174
I mean Marcus Aurelius has
written one of my favorite books,

00:25:20.174 --> 00:25:22.306
the Meditations of Marcus
Aurelius, and actually

00:25:22.306 --> 00:25:23.555
he didn't write it.

00:25:23.555 --> 00:25:26.292
Marcus Aurelius was a
Roman Emperor, and during

00:25:26.292 --> 00:25:29.181
a long military campaign,
in his journal, he wrote

00:25:29.181 --> 00:25:31.150
his thoughts and his
philosophies, and someone

00:25:31.150 --> 00:25:34.024
found those diaries, those
journals, and made them

00:25:34.024 --> 00:25:37.890
into this book, called the
Meditations of Marcus Aurelius.

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:39.933
And he does talk about
the shortness of life,

00:25:39.933 --> 00:25:42.210
and so does Epictetus, and a lot

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:44.801
of the great Stoic philosophers.

00:25:44.801 --> 00:25:48.517
And being in this cemetery,
and walking through it

00:25:48.517 --> 00:25:52.684
with great delicacy, it
reminded me how short life is.

00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:58.342
And it reminded me we all
have hopes and dreams,

00:25:58.342 --> 00:26:02.429
and we never know when those
dreams are going to get dashed.

00:26:02.429 --> 00:26:06.785
And we never know when
our last day will unfold.

00:26:06.785 --> 00:26:08.642
Some people will wake up
tomorrow morning, they'll get

00:26:08.642 --> 00:26:10.994
in their car, they'll
take a shower or they'll

00:26:10.994 --> 00:26:13.767
have their coffee, they'll say
goodbye to their loved ones,

00:26:13.767 --> 00:26:15.746
and that will be the last
day that their loved ones

00:26:15.746 --> 00:26:17.843
will ever see them.

00:26:17.843 --> 00:26:20.227
Some people on the planet
the day after tomorrow,

00:26:20.227 --> 00:26:22.952
will go into their doctor's
office to get the result

00:26:22.952 --> 00:26:26.276
of a routine checkup,
and the doctor will say,

00:26:26.276 --> 00:26:29.523
"Sit down, I've got
some sad news for you,"

00:26:29.523 --> 00:26:32.104
and they'll be diagnosed
with maybe some kind

00:26:32.104 --> 00:26:34.024
of a terminal illness.

00:26:34.024 --> 00:26:38.107
I mean, accidents, illness,
tragedy, catastrophe,

00:26:40.041 --> 00:26:43.200
is also a reality of humanity.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:47.194
And so, what I'm suggesting
to you is, do whatever

00:26:47.194 --> 00:26:50.911
it takes to be in the
moment, and to stay positive,

00:26:50.911 --> 00:26:54.565
and to stay on your game,
and to optimize your craft,

00:26:54.565 --> 00:26:56.330
and to spend time with your loved ones,

00:26:56.330 --> 00:26:58.730
and to take care of your
fitness, and to learn

00:26:58.730 --> 00:27:02.078
something new every day, and
to enjoy the beauty of sunsets

00:27:02.078 --> 00:27:05.344
and star-filled nights,
and at the same time,

00:27:05.344 --> 00:27:09.511
as much as possible, please,
do not lose perspective.

00:27:10.738 --> 00:27:13.488
Stay connected to your mortality.

00:27:14.365 --> 00:27:17.163
Steve Jobs in that famous Stanford speech,

00:27:17.163 --> 00:27:20.822
said it really well, "You are naked,

00:27:20.822 --> 00:27:23.019
"you have nothing to lose."

00:27:23.019 --> 00:27:26.710
And he learnt that when he
connected to his cancer.

00:27:26.710 --> 00:27:31.521
And so, maybe you will install
a ritual, once a month,

00:27:31.521 --> 00:27:36.377
on the way home from work, you
will pull over to a cemetery,

00:27:36.377 --> 00:27:40.349
and you'll spend five
minutes, even, in meditation,

00:27:40.349 --> 00:27:43.965
looking at the tombstones,
looking at the crosses,

00:27:43.965 --> 00:27:48.518
studying the graves, or
whatever your tradition is,

00:27:48.518 --> 00:27:51.957
in whatever nation you're from,
because not every tradition,

00:27:51.957 --> 00:27:54.043
I know, has the gravesites.

00:27:54.043 --> 00:27:58.043
Sometimes it's the funeral
pyre, which really...

00:28:03.081 --> 00:28:06.164
reminds me that life is really short,

00:28:08.757 --> 00:28:11.897
and you never know when your
last day is going to be.

00:28:11.897 --> 00:28:15.893
And so just build some kind
of a routine to remind you

00:28:15.893 --> 00:28:17.079
on the shortness of life.

00:28:17.079 --> 00:28:19.148
So that you tell the people you love

00:28:19.148 --> 00:28:22.248
how you feel about them,
so you write the letters

00:28:22.248 --> 00:28:23.764
that need to be written,
and I know people don't

00:28:23.764 --> 00:28:26.932
write letters, but writing
a letter to someone

00:28:26.932 --> 00:28:30.515
you need to express forgiveness
to, or gratitude to,

00:28:30.515 --> 00:28:32.909
is really powerful.

00:28:32.909 --> 00:28:36.409
Pursue the projects that will allow you to

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:39.747
be the legend of your industry.

00:28:39.747 --> 00:28:42.321
Chase the passions that fuel your joy.

00:28:42.321 --> 00:28:45.363
And live every day as if it was your last,

00:28:45.363 --> 00:28:49.530
because we never know when
our last day will show up.

