If there's someone
you'd like me to profile

00:28:57.068 --> 00:28:59.625
in their Future Top 10,
please check out the link

00:28:59.625 --> 00:29:02.414
down in the description below,
and you can cast your vote

00:29:02.414 --> 00:29:04.249
on who we should do next.

00:29:04.249 --> 00:29:06.039
I also want to give a
quick shoutout to Jude.

00:29:06.039 --> 00:29:07.751
Jude, thank you so much
for pickin' up a copy

00:29:07.751 --> 00:29:10.822
of my book, Your One Word, and
taking that awesome picture,

00:29:10.822 --> 00:29:12.141
and sharing it with me.

00:29:12.141 --> 00:29:13.747
I really really appreciate the support,

00:29:13.747 --> 00:29:15.278
and I hope you enjoyed the read.

00:29:15.278 --> 00:29:16.655
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:29:16.655 --> 00:29:17.765
I believe in you.

00:29:17.765 --> 00:29:19.616
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:29:19.616 --> 00:29:22.740
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:24.067
I'll see you soon.

00:29:24.067 --> 00:29:25.249
(swooshing)

00:29:25.249 --> 00:29:26.494
(booming)

00:29:26.494 --> 00:29:28.827
(swooshing)

00:29:30.181 --> 00:29:31.848
- Run your own race.

00:29:32.942 --> 00:29:35.654
And that's really the
first lesson I learned from

00:29:35.654 --> 00:29:37.044
one of my mentors.

00:29:37.044 --> 00:29:38.609
Run your own race.

00:29:38.609 --> 00:29:41.007
Stay in your own lane.

00:29:41.007 --> 00:29:45.174
You have no competition, if
you are in your own division.

00:29:46.811 --> 00:29:48.191
And what I mean by that is simply this.

00:29:48.191 --> 00:29:50.580
It's so easy to get swept off your game,

00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:53.802
swept off your vision,
swept off your values,

00:29:53.802 --> 00:29:57.478
swept off your focus, when
you're measuring your success,

00:29:57.478 --> 00:30:00.458
and your performance,
by your so-called peers

00:30:00.458 --> 00:30:04.661
in your industry, or your
competition within your craft.

00:30:04.661 --> 00:30:06.967
And that first lesson was
so profound, he simply said,

00:30:06.967 --> 00:30:08.442
"Run your own race."

00:30:08.442 --> 00:30:09.275
You know?

00:30:09.275 --> 00:30:11.967
Come up with your ambition,
come up with your vision,

00:30:11.967 --> 00:30:16.197
and have this singular focus,
and the character discipline,

00:30:16.197 --> 00:30:18.684
to say I'm just going
to put these blinders on

00:30:18.684 --> 00:30:20.769
and stay true to myself,
true to my values,

00:30:20.769 --> 00:30:22.109
true to my vision.

00:30:22.109 --> 00:30:25.043
So I've been living my life, recently,

00:30:25.043 --> 00:30:28.965
under one compelling
standard, and simply this.

00:30:28.965 --> 00:30:33.132
Be only with people, do
only the work, and be only

00:30:35.667 --> 00:30:39.250
in the spaces and
places, that fuel my joy.

00:30:40.501 --> 00:30:43.738
I think life is too short to be depleted.

00:30:43.738 --> 00:30:46.397
And so I've spent a lot
of my life helping people

00:30:46.397 --> 00:30:49.376
rise to iconic as
producers, and performers,

00:30:49.376 --> 00:30:52.389
and creatives, and
entrepreneurs, and leaders.

00:30:52.389 --> 00:30:54.187
And so what I wanted to do
in this mastery session,

00:30:54.187 --> 00:30:56.149
is really share with you
some of the things I do

00:30:56.149 --> 00:31:00.435
in my own life to fuel
my joy, fuel my energy,

00:31:00.435 --> 00:31:02.546
fuel my creativity.

00:31:02.546 --> 00:31:06.176
And what I'm really speaking
of here, is adventure.

00:31:06.176 --> 00:31:09.353
So if you talk to any good neurobiologist,

00:31:09.353 --> 00:31:13.170
they will tell you that
the brain craves novelty.

00:31:13.170 --> 00:31:16.837
The way we are hard-wired,
is to pursue joy,

00:31:17.906 --> 00:31:21.815
to pursue novelty, to pursue newness.

00:31:21.815 --> 00:31:23.887
And that's why, so often,
we start something,

00:31:23.887 --> 00:31:25.047
but we really don't finish it.

00:31:25.047 --> 00:31:28.592
We're always looking
for that one new thing,

00:31:28.592 --> 00:31:32.075
that will fuel our joy, fuel our ideation,

00:31:32.075 --> 00:31:33.159
fuel our creativity.

00:31:33.159 --> 00:31:37.093
It's almost like, we've got
this inner child within us,

00:31:37.093 --> 00:31:40.312
constantly longing to
see the light of day.

00:31:40.312 --> 00:31:41.586
And you know adults are nothing more

00:31:41.586 --> 00:31:44.405
than deteriorated children,
you never want to lose

00:31:44.405 --> 00:31:48.572
that connection with that
festive, fiery, sparkling,

00:31:49.994 --> 00:31:51.738
creative, playful inner child.

00:31:51.738 --> 00:31:54.428
I mean, the more you can
connect with that inner child,

00:31:54.428 --> 00:31:55.261
the more you're going to find

00:31:55.261 --> 00:31:59.549
your productivity growing
exponentially, your creativity

00:31:59.549 --> 00:32:01.944
firing up, and basically
you're just going out

00:32:01.944 --> 00:32:03.834
into your industry,
and out into the world,

00:32:03.834 --> 00:32:06.209
and radiating positivity.

00:32:06.209 --> 00:32:08.909
So, what are some of the things
that I do do fuel my joy,

00:32:08.909 --> 00:32:11.823
and to keep that sense of adventure alive,

00:32:11.823 --> 00:32:15.249
so I don't lose the sparkle in my eye?

00:32:15.249 --> 00:32:16.082
Traveling.

00:32:16.082 --> 00:32:17.871
I just love being on airplanes.

00:32:17.871 --> 00:32:20.029
Fundamentally, I am a nomad.

00:32:20.029 --> 00:32:23.820
So, I love being in Rome
and eating the pasta,

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:27.247
and experiencing the fresh
mozzarella di bufala,

00:32:27.247 --> 00:32:31.880
or the burrata, watching
the way the light falls

00:32:31.880 --> 00:32:33.001
over the architecture.

00:32:33.001 --> 00:32:35.653
I just love Rome so much.

00:32:35.653 --> 00:32:37.578
I love getting up in the morning,

00:32:37.578 --> 00:32:42.265
while the Romans are asleep,
and running up to Monti,

00:32:42.265 --> 00:32:46.592
and then past the Colosseum,
and then past Trevi Fountain.

00:32:46.592 --> 00:32:49.573
I mean, that is one of the
greatest runs, I think,

00:32:49.573 --> 00:32:52.145
on the planet, at least for me.

00:32:52.145 --> 00:32:53.350
I love going to Mauritius.

00:32:53.350 --> 00:32:54.898
Why do I love Mauritius?

00:32:54.898 --> 00:32:59.464
It's this island paradise,
where no-one can find me,

00:32:59.464 --> 00:33:03.581
and I can immerse myself in
creativity, and I can refuel.

00:33:03.581 --> 00:33:06.739
You talk about adventure,
going to Mauritius

00:33:06.739 --> 00:33:09.478
and swimming with the
dolphins, is one of the things

00:33:09.478 --> 00:33:13.813
you need to do on your before
I die list, before you die.

00:33:13.813 --> 00:33:16.711
I just love places like Stockholm.

00:33:16.711 --> 00:33:18.252
I love Latin America.

00:33:18.252 --> 00:33:19.581
I love the Middle East.

00:33:19.581 --> 00:33:20.849
I adore Europe.

00:33:20.849 --> 00:33:22.733
I love London.

00:33:22.733 --> 00:33:25.981
I love Switzerland, because I love to ski.

00:33:25.981 --> 00:33:27.736
I love going to India.

00:33:27.736 --> 00:33:30.281
I love going to Southeast Asia.

00:33:30.281 --> 00:33:31.873
I love traveling the planet.

00:33:31.873 --> 00:33:33.571
Because here's the thing,
I don't think there is

00:33:33.571 --> 00:33:37.738
a better education known to
humankind, than traveling.

00:33:38.645 --> 00:33:41.497
Michelangelo is really a great story

00:33:41.497 --> 00:33:44.080
on willpower, and self-control.

00:33:45.436 --> 00:33:48.032
He was beaten severely
when he was a young kid

00:33:48.032 --> 00:33:49.353
by his father.

00:33:49.353 --> 00:33:51.829
His father wanted him to be-- to pursue

00:33:51.829 --> 00:33:55.626
a more noble profession, a
more lucrative profession.

00:33:55.626 --> 00:33:59.109
And Michelangelo wanted to be a sculptor.

00:33:59.109 --> 00:34:01.824
And his father beat him,
and his father tried to s--

00:34:01.824 --> 00:34:02.861
to get him off course.

00:34:02.861 --> 00:34:05.146
And Michelangelo said, "No,
I want to be a sculptor."

00:34:05.146 --> 00:34:08.260
And finally, through his
grit and perseverance,

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:11.315
and unrelenting-ness,
Michelangelo's father said,

00:34:11.315 --> 00:34:12.328
"You know what, okay fine.

00:34:12.328 --> 00:34:14.153
"You're 14 years old,
I'm going to allow you

00:34:14.153 --> 00:34:18.170
"to go to apprentice
with a Florentine master,

00:34:18.170 --> 00:34:21.415
"and he will help you become a sculptor."

00:34:21.415 --> 00:34:24.574
And Michelangelo moved to
Florence, and he started to study,

00:34:24.574 --> 00:34:25.763
and he started to practice.

00:34:25.763 --> 00:34:27.807
And again, this is a
story of self-discipline,

00:34:27.807 --> 00:34:30.047
and world-class willpower.

00:34:30.047 --> 00:34:32.090
And through his mastery,
and through his practice,

00:34:32.090 --> 00:34:34.169
and through his suffering,
and through his persistence,

00:34:34.169 --> 00:34:37.570
and through his focus, all
of these great lost traits,

00:34:37.570 --> 00:34:40.280
that are no longer
celebrated in this world

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:43.222
where all-too-often, people
are card-carrying members

00:34:43.222 --> 00:34:45.309
of the cult of mediocrity.

00:34:45.309 --> 00:34:48.522
Through this practice,
through this devotion,

00:34:48.522 --> 00:34:49.961
he became well-known.

00:34:49.961 --> 00:34:51.537
He became known far and wide.

00:34:51.537 --> 00:34:54.922
He created that-- the
beautiful sculpture, David.

00:34:54.922 --> 00:34:57.252
If you go to Museum of
Accademia in Florence,

00:34:57.252 --> 00:34:58.473
you will see David.

00:34:58.473 --> 00:35:00.724
You will see some of
his other masterpieces.

00:35:00.724 --> 00:35:04.025
And, part of that whole
region started to honor

00:35:04.025 --> 00:35:06.978
the way that Michelangelo
was appearing and performing

00:35:06.978 --> 00:35:08.145
as a sculptor.

00:35:09.074 --> 00:35:11.603
Because of his mastery, a
lot of the other artists,

00:35:11.603 --> 00:35:13.880
like Raphael, and the
Renaissance painters,

00:35:13.880 --> 00:35:16.515
became-- they became jealous.

00:35:16.515 --> 00:35:19.988
And so they devised a very
elaborate and interesting plan

00:35:19.988 --> 00:35:23.488
to discredit and to ridicule Michelangelo.

00:35:25.100 --> 00:35:28.193
And, Michelangelo's
peers went to the Pope,

00:35:28.193 --> 00:35:30.264
who was Julius II.

00:35:30.264 --> 00:35:32.271
And they said to the Pope,
"Why don't you get Michelangelo

00:35:32.271 --> 00:35:34.802
"to paint the Sistine Chapel?"

00:35:34.802 --> 00:35:37.760
And the Pope said, "Sounds good."

00:35:37.760 --> 00:35:41.362
And so the Pope went over
the Michelangelo, and said,

00:35:41.362 --> 00:35:45.112
"I want you to restore
the greatness of Rome.

00:35:45.975 --> 00:35:49.387
"And the greatness of Rome
is now on your shoulders,

00:35:49.387 --> 00:35:52.641
"because I want you to
paint the Sistine Chapel.

00:35:52.641 --> 00:35:54.218
"And I want you to make it a masterpiece

00:35:54.218 --> 00:35:57.765
"that holds the world in
awe when they see it."

00:35:57.765 --> 00:36:00.292
And Michelangelo replied
to the Pope, he said,

00:36:00.292 --> 00:36:04.459
"You know, I'm not a painter,
and I've never painted,

00:36:05.904 --> 00:36:08.109
"I am a sculptor."

00:36:08.109 --> 00:36:10.822
But Julius II was very persistent,

00:36:10.822 --> 00:36:12.332
and eventually Michelangelo said,

00:36:12.332 --> 00:36:13.765
"Fine, I'm going to agree."

00:36:13.765 --> 00:36:18.182
And over four years, four
hard years, four of the years

00:36:18.182 --> 00:36:21.880
that took incredible
self-control, incredible grit,

00:36:21.880 --> 00:36:23.643
we're going to talk about
grit in this webinar,

00:36:23.643 --> 00:36:26.623
incredible focus, incredible discipline.

00:36:26.623 --> 00:36:29.956
Michelangelo created the Sistine Chapel.

00:36:30.872 --> 00:36:32.504
Now, let's put this into perspective.

00:36:32.504 --> 00:36:35.517
I mean, Michelangelo had
never painted fresco before,

00:36:35.517 --> 00:36:39.270
Michelangelo had to paint
something on the ceiling

00:36:39.270 --> 00:36:42.439
of the Sistine Chapel that
people standing many feet below

00:36:42.439 --> 00:36:45.249
could look up at and
see with great detail.

00:36:45.249 --> 00:36:48.330
Michelangelo dealt with
the fact that the painting

00:36:48.330 --> 00:36:52.721
on the Sistine Chapel was
moldy, there were paint issues.

00:36:52.721 --> 00:36:54.839
He had to deal with the fact
that the Pope was very ill,

00:36:54.839 --> 00:36:57.221
and he thought he might not get paid.

00:36:57.221 --> 00:37:01.008
He actually developed this
incredible, elaborate system

00:37:01.008 --> 00:37:03.350
of scaffolding, which
allowed him to paint--

00:37:03.350 --> 00:37:07.517
to paint backwards, for four
years, on the Sistine Chapel,

00:37:08.936 --> 00:37:11.357
and he damaged his back in the process.

00:37:11.357 --> 00:37:13.984
But he did it for the art,
he did it for the discipline,

00:37:13.984 --> 00:37:15.901
he did it for what it made of him,

00:37:15.901 --> 00:37:18.953
he did it for his commitment,
because the great artists,

00:37:18.953 --> 00:37:22.099
and the great performers
are much less about ego,

00:37:22.099 --> 00:37:25.664
and applause, and money,
and they do their work

00:37:25.664 --> 00:37:29.320
for what it makes of them as human beings.

00:37:29.320 --> 00:37:32.170
And after four years,
Michelangelo's eyesight

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:34.934
was so damaged from looking at the images

00:37:34.934 --> 00:37:36.681
on the Sistine Chapel, that he

00:37:36.681 --> 00:37:38.145
suffered permanent eye damage.

00:37:38.145 --> 00:37:40.241
But like I said, once he was finished,

00:37:40.241 --> 00:37:43.824
the world was awestruck
at what he created.

00:37:45.082 --> 00:37:47.825
And now, 20 million people
a year go to the Vatican,

00:37:47.825 --> 00:37:52.253
and they do experience this
breath-taking work of artistry,

00:37:52.253 --> 00:37:56.430
and human ingenuity,
constructed by an ordinary man,

00:37:56.430 --> 00:38:01.161
who exercised world-class
willpower, named Michelangelo.

00:38:01.161 --> 00:38:03.193
And so, ladies and gentlemen,
I guess what I'm suggesting

00:38:03.193 --> 00:38:06.895
to you, is one of fundamental points.

00:38:06.895 --> 00:38:09.421
The myth of the talented genius,

00:38:09.421 --> 00:38:12.597
the myth of the gifted
genius, is nothing more

00:38:12.597 --> 00:38:15.264
than a lie society has told you.

00:38:17.142 --> 00:38:18.651
I don't know if I expressed that clearly,

00:38:18.651 --> 00:38:20.246
but what I'm trying to suggest to you is,

00:38:20.246 --> 00:38:23.665
the world says, the
Michelangelos, and the Beckhams,

00:38:23.665 --> 00:38:25.832
and the Jay-Zs, and the...

00:38:30.629 --> 00:38:33.507
the great entrepreneurs, the Carlos Slims,

00:38:33.507 --> 00:38:37.477
the Warren Buffetts, the
Jack Dorseys, you know

00:38:37.477 --> 00:38:40.123
the founder of Twitter, the Jeff Bezoses,

00:38:40.123 --> 00:38:43.748
the founder of Amazon, the
Mozarts, and the Picassos,

00:38:43.748 --> 00:38:44.915
and the Bachs.

00:38:46.016 --> 00:38:49.508
Society teaches us, and
trains us, that these people

00:38:49.508 --> 00:38:52.675
are somehow born with special talents.

00:38:53.764 --> 00:38:56.085
And what I'm here to remind
you, with deep respect

00:38:56.085 --> 00:39:00.085
for all 25,000, or 24,922
of you on this webinar

00:39:01.133 --> 00:39:03.662
from around the world, is that is a lie

00:39:03.662 --> 00:39:07.245
that society has sold
us, to keep us small.

00:39:08.702 --> 00:39:11.500
And if you look at the
science, and science coming out

00:39:11.500 --> 00:39:13.334
right now is indisputable, we're going to

00:39:13.334 --> 00:39:14.802
get into that science.

00:39:14.802 --> 00:39:19.799
But what science says, is
genius is much less about--

00:39:19.799 --> 00:39:22.716
about your genetics, and much more about

00:39:22.716 --> 00:39:26.301
your self-control, and your discipline.

00:39:26.301 --> 00:39:29.458
Genius is much less about natural talent,

00:39:29.458 --> 00:39:33.625
and much more about practice,
persistence, focus, grit,

00:39:35.718 --> 00:39:38.620
dealing with suffering,
dealing with failure,

00:39:38.620 --> 00:39:41.257
dealing with laughter,
while the rest of the world

00:39:41.257 --> 00:39:43.539
thinks you should stop,
genius is much more

00:39:43.539 --> 00:39:47.266
about continuing, practice,
developing your craft.

00:39:47.266 --> 00:39:48.099
(swooshing)

